    {
      "@id": "https://theflatratemovers.com/transloading-at-the-virginia-inland-port/#faq",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Your container arrives by rail from Hampton Roads and the Port Authority lifts it at Front Royal (1). From there the freight is ours to handle: destuffed, counted and sorted, rebuilt as palletized truck freight or split loads, and handed to the onward leg — a carrier pickup we arrange, our leased trucks, or storage until your receiver is ready (5, 6)."
          },
          "name": "What happens to my freight at the Virginia Inland Port?"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "When the container stops being the right unit: your receiver takes pallets and the box is floor-loaded; one box serves several destinations; three ocean containers consolidate into two domestic trailers; or the equipment clock makes an early empty worth more than a long inland ride (5). If your dock can take the sealed box as it arrives, you do not need a transload."
          },
          "name": "When is transloading better than delivering the container intact?"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. The port is a U.S. Customs-designated port of entry; its ground in the Warren County Industrial Corridor is covered by FTZ 20 magnet sites and Warren County also lies within FTZ 185’s service area — and those positions belong to the port (1, 4). We are not customs brokers and operate no bonded warehouse or FTZ — your entry is filed by you or your broker. Ours is the freight handling around those processes, including the handling a directed exam move requires (5, 6)."
          },
          "name": "Do you clear customs, or operate inside the free trade zone?"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes — that is freight rework, published on its own page. Salvageable freight is separated, counted and restacked; damaged freight is set aside and reported as found; the rebuilt load is repalletized, rewrapped, braced and banded to the federal securement standard at 49 CFR 393 before it moves (3, 7). Emergency and after-hours response is available at premium rates agreed before dispatch; no rate is published (7)."
          },
          "name": "My load shifted and the receiver refused it. Can you rebuild it?"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Per job, at one flat rate agreed in writing before dispatch — never a per-container schedule. The factors are the scope: the mode pair, floor-loaded or palletized arrival, piece count, sort depth, destination split, storage days, load condition and clock pressure (5, 6). Third-party charges the scope causes pass through at documented cost, itemized on the same invoice."
          },
          "name": "How is transloading priced?"
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Refrigerated and cold-chain handling sits outside this service, and so do tank, bulk-liquids, hazardous-materials and oversize heavy-haul programs (5, 6). The terminal's own capabilities are the Port Authority's to describe (1); our service is the dry-freight handling chain in the transloading datasets."
          },
          "name": "Do you handle refrigerated or hazardous freight at the Inland Port?"
        }
      ]
    },
